From bc97ddf2acbbc2561deaf9fdb81328fdc354e2c9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zed-patches <zed-patches@localhost>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 18:20:44 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_ui: Up and Down in the composer recall the thread's
 sent messages

Resending or tweaking an earlier prompt meant scrolling up, copying it and
pasting it back. A shell solves this with Up/Down history and the composer had
those keys free, so it now has a per-thread history: Up recalls the operator's
earlier messages in this thread, newest first; Down walks back and finally
restores the draft that was in the composer when browsing started -- an empty
one included (story 018).

**What counts as a sent message.** The operator's own `UserMessage` entries of
the current thread, re-read from `AcpThread::entries()` on every fresh Up, so a
message sent since the last browse is the first one offered and a reopened
thread's replayed messages and its live ones form one history. Not counted:
`indented` entries (a subagent's prompt rendered inside the thread), agent
output, and queued messages, which are not thread entries at all. It does NOT
filter on `is_optimistic`: a live-sent message stays optimistic for good when
the agent does not echo it, so that flag says nothing about whether it was
sent. A message sent twice is two steps (no shell-style de-duplication), and an
entry with no content to rebuild is skipped (logged at DEBUG with the session
id and entry index).

**The caret rules -- editing is never hijacked.** `up` and `down` are bound in
`AcpThread > Editor` to `agent::RecallOlderMessage` / `RecallNewerMessage`.
Up recalls only with a single caret, nothing selected, on the composer's first
DISPLAY row (so a wrapped first line still moves the caret); Down only while
browsing and on the last row. Everywhere else the handler calls
`cx.propagate()`, and GPUI then tries the next binding for the same keystroke
-- `editor::MoveUp`/`MoveDown`, or vim's -- so caret movement is exactly what it
was. Menus win before either: `Editor && showing_completions` (completions,
@-mentions, slash commands), `showing_signature_help` and `Picker > Editor`
come later in the keymap at the same depth, and `context_menu_visible()` is a
second guard. Upstream's Up-in-an-empty-composer, which pulls the last QUEUED
message back, keeps priority: with a non-empty queue and an empty composer the
recall propagates to it. An older step leaves the caret at the start and a
newer one at the end, so repeated Up/Down walk straight through a multi-line
message instead of stopping on each of its lines.

**The draft rule.** Browsing starts by keeping the composer's content
(`draft_content_blocks_snapshot`, so mentions survive) and ends three ways:
Down past the newest message restores it; any edit of a recalled message ends
browsing, so the edited text is the new draft and the next Up starts from the
newest again; and any operator message entering the thread ends it. That is one
end point, on the thread's new-entry event, rather than one call per send path:
the composer, the message queue (Send Now and the automatic send at the end of
a turn) and resubmitting an edited earlier message (`regenerate`) all arrive
there. When the message did not come from the composer, the recalled text in
the composer was never the operator's, so the saved draft is put back; the
composer's own send ends browsing before it clears the composer, so its entry
finds nothing to undo. A recalled index that no longer names a sent message
also ends browsing with the draft put back. The recall's own replacement is
told apart from an operator edit by the composer buffer's version, captured
right after the recall -- every edit `set_source_message` makes is synchronous.
While browsing, the thread's persisted `draft_prompt` is left alone: a recalled
message is not the draft, and switching thread or quitting mid-browse must not
lose the one being written. Known and accepted: the snapshot trims leading and
trailing whitespace, and a mention still loading comes back as a plain link.

Recalled messages are rebuilt with `set_source_message(source_blocks)`, the
same reconstruction the edit-message action uses, so mentions and images come
back as they were sent. No recall in a read-only composer (a draft with
unsupported content -- the same refusal `move_queued_message_to_main_editor`
makes) or while a send is resolving its contents.

**Keymaps.** linux, macos and windows each get the block; vim.json gets one
under `MessageEditor > Editor && VimControl && !menu`, because vim.json loads
after the defaults and `vim::Up`/`vim::Down` would otherwise shadow recall in
normal mode, which is the composer's starting mode there.

The model is a pure `SentMessageHistory` in `conversation_view/sent_history.rs`
(entry indices plus the saved draft), held per `ThreadView`. Tests: 10 unit
tests for the model, 22 GPUI tests driving the real keymap with real keystrokes
(`recall_*`).

Pure Zed and generic ACP: it rides `claude-agent-acp-plus` BY CHOICE, like
0014 and 0026, and needs no adapter feature.
---

